#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:5
#: 4968a789f219430aab748c75ff2bdc78
msgid "Stall Prevention"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:7
#: 2065da8648844804b859d91348e937b3
msgid "Plane has logic to try to prevent stalls when in certain modes. This page describes how it works and how to configure it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:11
#: ef90cf33faaa420b9cb05299c6bc5550
msgid "Basic Idea"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:13
#: dc8203e491544bc9bf003b2a7687415d
msgid "One of the most common ways that users crash is through a stall. A stall happens when the airflow over the wing is not sufficient to hold the aircraft in the air. Stalls can happen at any speed, but the most common type of stall is a low speed stall, where the airflow is too slow to provide enough lift."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:19
#: 9aa98067b3434bb3b846b0e07ee1453b
msgid "The amount of airflow you need over the wing to hold the aircraft in the air depends (among other things) on the bank angle you are flying at. If you are banked over hard then you need to fly faster to get enough lift to stay in the air. This is because the lift is produced perpendicular to the wing, so when the wing is rolled over it provides only part of the lift to holding the aircraft up, and the rest of the lift goes into making the aircraft turn."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:27
#: bdf1a94abb8d4e7da9228e548ea9439b
msgid "When the :ref:`STALL_PREVENTION <STALL_PREVENTION>` parameter is set to 1 then two things are done:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:30
#: 08c10100b7bd4452bf70818013188ed1
msgid "when in roll controlled modes the autopilot will monitor your demanded bank angle and airspeed and work out if you have sufficient margin above the stall speed to turn at the demanded bank angle. If you don't then the turn will be limited to the safe limit, but it will always allow a bank of at least 25 degrees (to ensure you can still manoeuvre if your airspeed estimate is badly off)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:36
#: bfc5b9b16ac34da6a0df980139dbbbb4
msgid "when in auto-throttle modes the autopilot will also raise the minimum airspeed in the :ref:`TECS system <tecs-total-energy-control-system-for-speed-height-tuning-guide>` to the level at which the current demanded bank angle can be safely achieved. So it will add more engine power or lower the nose to raise the airspeed so that the bank angle that the navigation controller is demanding can be achieved without a stall"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:44
#: ce5f7d2ae44c4126afcb9d9cbb527118
msgid "Configuring stall prevention"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:46
#: 5d669793ff6d4043a8db4652192462d8
msgid "There are two key parameters that control stall prevention:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:48
#: 0cdc70c55c1549598cab788fe4ec3b32
msgid "The :ref:`STALL_PREVENTION <STALL_PREVENTION>` parameter. If this is set to zero then no stall prevention is done. This may be useful if you have no airspeed sensor and the synthetic airspeed estimate is not good enough"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/stall-prevention.rst:52
#: 19e4e48ac5aa494289c90caf57edea42
msgid "The :ref:`AIRSPEED_STALL<AIRSPEED_STALL>` parameter. If stall prevention is enabled this speed is used to calculate the minimum airspeed while banking. If this is set to 0 then the stall speed is assumed to be the :ref:`AIRSPEED_MIN<AIRSPEED_MIN>`. Typically it is set slightly higher then true stall speed. It is this value that is scaled with the bank angle to calculate the safe airspeed for any demanded bank angle."
msgstr ""

